00:03:16.492 --> 00:03:22.132
The time says court action and Democrats coming control of Congress could still stop the plans.

00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:27.968
Ottawa is where Mark Carney tables his second federal budget on Thursday, the CBC reports.

00:03:28.528 --> 00:03:31.908
The next step in a plan aimed at an affordability crunch in the trade war.

00:03:32.348 --> 00:03:34.768
He has been prime minister since March 2025.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:39.268
Coverage in the Globe and Mail says finance minister Francois Philippe Champagne

00:03:39.268 --> 00:03:42.848
has said the budget will focus on affordability and cultural sovereignty.

00:03:42.848 --> 00:03:48.088
Jake Fuss, Director of Fiscal Studies at the Fraser Institute, argues in that paper

00:03:48.088 --> 00:03:50.988
that Canadian should expect more spending in large deficits.

00:03:51.488 --> 00:03:54.888
He writes that Carney promised a very different approach from Justin Trudeau.

00:03:55.288 --> 00:03:58.328
Later said he would be spending less, and then increased spending.

00:03:58.748 --> 00:04:04.448
Fuss says the government posted a $66.9 billion deficit for 2025 to 2026.

00:04:04.928 --> 00:04:11.788
Project $65.3 billion for 2026 to 2027, and deficits of at least $53 billion in each of

00:04:11.788 --> 00:04:13.008
the four years after that.

00:04:13.488 --> 00:04:17.408
Added up from 2025-26 through 20-29-30, he writes,

00:04:17.928 --> 00:04:20.828
those deficits are double what Trudeau plan before leaving office.

00:04:21.308 --> 00:04:24.488
He notes Ottawa has not balanced a budget in nearly 20 years.

00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:27.248
After the Trudeau government borrowed hundreds of billions

00:04:27.248 --> 00:04:30.128
and pushed inflation-adjusted spending in debt per person

00:04:30.128 --> 00:04:31.148
to historic highs.

00:04:31.668 --> 00:04:33.328
Borrowing is how the gap gets covered.

00:04:33.788 --> 00:04:37.428
Fuss says total federal debt will pass a projected $2.9 trillion

00:04:37.428 --> 00:04:39.128
by 20-29-30.

00:04:39.128 --> 00:04:45.468
Almost 300 billion more than Trudeau planned, and that interest will hit a projected $75.7 billion.

00:04:46.068 --> 00:04:49.788
More than employment insurance and the Canada Child Benefit combined that year.

00:04:50.268 --> 00:04:55.688
He argues younger Canadians are the ones likely to face higher taxes, fewer services, or both.

00:04:56.088 --> 00:04:59.628
He also argues federal borrowing competes with families and businesses,

00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:07.828
so lenders charge more for mortgages, cars, and renovations, and that today's higher rates exist in part because of that federal and provincial borrowing.

00:05:07.828 --> 00:05:13.768
Last year, he writes, the government split day-to-day operating spending, including old age security

00:05:13.768 --> 00:05:18.508
and employment insurance from capital spending, defined as anything that builds an asset,

00:05:18.928 --> 00:05:24.828
and said last month it would balance the operating budget by 2028 to 2029. Both categories hit

00:05:24.828 --> 00:05:30.028
the bottom line, he says, and the split hides the true books. The Parliamentary Budget Officer has

00:05:30.028 --> 00:05:35.488
criticized that capital classification, he writes, because items can be shifted from operating to capital.

00:05:35.488 --> 00:05:39.948
A separate PBO report last month said the government is not on track to balance the

00:05:39.948 --> 00:05:42.768
operating budget by 2028 to 29 anyway.

00:05:43.228 --> 00:05:47.868
His conclusion is that without deep spending cuts or a huge rise in revenue, the overall

00:05:47.868 --> 00:05:52.348
deficit stays larger than Trudeau's projections, and younger Canadians inherit the bill.

